extras={
'README.md':'''The accepted four-case suite loads the real guards and error classes too. That matters because an unassigned buyer fails the earlier viewing guard, while a broker can view the transaction but still cannot submit an offer. The course asks learners to predict this difference before running the tests. The source remains unchanged; repository adapters make successful arguments and failure stopping points observable without asserting that the backing store is transactional.''',
'01-CODEBASE-MAP.md':'''Follow [guards.ts](../src/lib/permissions/guards.ts) before jumping to the offer policy. requireAggregate first asks the repository for a visible aggregate, raises 404 if absent, and then invokes requireTransactionViewer. Only after that succeeds does submitOffer apply requireOfferSubmitter. The success path enriches the request with submittedById from the session, so a client cannot choose that field through the declared input. It then sends OFFER_SUBMITTED to the status method and records offer.submitted with amount metadata. The returned object is the repository's offer. No transaction wrapper groups these calls, and submitOffer does not invoke the workflow validator used by updateStatus.''',
'02-CONCEPTS.md':'''Viewing and mutating are separate permissions. The real policy allows brokers to view any transaction, but the offer rule still requires the assigned BUYER. An unrelated buyer is refused by visibility before the offer guard runs. A useful test therefore checks real guard composition, not just a standalone boolean. There is also a persistence distinction: createOffer is a repository port method, not proof of an external provider operation. A failure from that method stops later calls; a failure from status comes after offer creation was attempted. Sequential awaits enforce order but cannot roll back a previous repository write by themselves.''',
'03-WORKED-CHANGE.md':'''The initial draft substituted the viewer guard and recreated the offer guard around a separately loaded policy. Root review replaced those substitutes with the real modules. The successful test now asserts exact transaction id, submitter id, status and audit payload, as well as returned-object identity. Negative cases check real statusCode 403 and code FORBIDDEN. A new missing-aggregate case checks the real 404 shape. The fault matrix injects errors at create, status and audit and requires the same original error to propagate. This is a worked improvement in test fidelity, not a change to application behavior or a claim that every transaction workflow is correct.''',
'04-TESTING-AND-DEBUGGING.md':'''The accepted command runs four named cases. Each uses a fresh fixture with an ordered call ledger, replacing shared counters that could conceal ordering mistakes. Actual TransactionService, permission guards, policies and error classes execute; only the injected repository port is substituted. Start debugging at the first unexpected ledger entry. If status follows a rejected create, an await or error boundary changed. If a broker succeeds, inspect the offer guard rather than viewer policy. If the output id differs, check whether the service stopped returning the original offer. A passing ledger cannot establish database rollback, network delivery or request-schema validation.''',
'05-PRACTICE.md':'''Score two points each for locating the viewer gate, locating the offer gate, predicting successful arguments, identifying failure stopping points and naming persistence limits. Add an assigned AGENT to the role matrix: it can view the transaction but must still fail offer submission. Next, inject an error into updateTransactionStatus and list which operation already ran. Compare that with a missing aggregate and explain why both failures reject the promise but have different side-effect risks. For a senior design exercise, propose a unit of work around offer, status and audit, then identify a real storage test needed to prove rollback rather than only callback ordering.''',
'06-SOLUTIONS-AND-REVIEW.md':'''The assigned agent and broker pass viewing but fail offer submission; an unrelated buyer fails viewing first. All stop before creation. Missing repository visibility returns 404, whereas a returned aggregate rejected by a real guard produces 403. Successful creation receives submittedById=buyer-1, then status becomes OFFER_SUBMITTED and audit records the same actor plus amount. Creation failure stops before status; status failure follows create but precedes audit; audit failure follows both earlier attempts. The real service does not reverse any of them. Full credit requires saying attempted writes rather than durable commits, since the repository in the lab stores no durable data.''',
'07-TRACE-LAB.md':'''Add these rows to an ordered worksheet:

| Actor or fault | Last call | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Assigned buyer | Audit | Original offer returned |
| Unassigned buyer | Lookup | Viewer refusal |
| Broker or assigned agent | Lookup | Offer refusal |
| Missing aggregate | Lookup | Not found |
| Create rejects | Create | Same error |
| Status rejects | Status | Same error, no audit |
| Audit rejects | Audit | Same error |

The rows distinguish failure location from outcome type. Draw the two guards between lookup and create, then explain why no successful later callback proves that earlier effects were rolled back. Compare your worksheet with the exact argument assertions in the runtime file.''',
'VERIFICATION.md':'''The accepted root label is crud-b05-astra-runtime and runs four cases using actual service, viewer/offer guards, policy and errors. Repository adapters record exact arguments and ordered attempts; no external provider is contacted. The creation/status/audit failure matrix preserves error identity and stops later calls. No production behavior was changed, and the unchanged service is excluded from the delivery manifest; the snapshot remains a reading reference. This lab does not verify lifecycle legality of submission from every transaction status. The separate workflow validator is used by updateStatus, not submitOffer. Native routes, authorization middleware, persistent repositories and rollback remain unexecuted.'''
}
